<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Karl Philipp zu Schwarzenberg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzenberg">House of Schwarzenberg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg, was an Austrian field marshal and diplomat who commanded the Austrian auxiliary corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the Sixth Coalition in 1813 he became commander-in-chief of the allied armies, which defeated Napoleon at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in October 1813 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg.jpg" alt="Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel_Filip_Schwarzenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karl Philipp was born on 18 or 19 April 1771 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, one of thirteen children of Johann Nepomuk Anton of Schwarzenberg and Marie Eleonore, Countess of Öttingen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a>; seven of the children did not reach adulthood. The family held land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and was close to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, and in 1788 he entered the Austrian army as a lieutenant. In 1789 he fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the Austro-Turkish War, and he became a major in 1792.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the French Revolutionary War Schwarzenberg served in the advance guard of Prince Josias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> in 1793. At Le Cateau-Cambrésis in 1794 his charge at the head of his regiment, supported by twelve British squadrons, broke a French corps of around 25,000 men and captured 32 guns, and he was decorated with the Knight's Cross of the Military Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. He became a general-major in 1796 and a Generalleutnant in 1799. In 1809 he led a cavalry division at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a>, which the Austrians lost.</p><p>In 1812, after the Treaty of Paris made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he took an Austrian corps of around 30,000 men to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, trying to satisfy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> without angering Russia. His troops won at Gorodetschna and Wolkowisk, and in late November withdrew into winter quarters at Bialystok under a verbal agreement with the Russians. Of the 30,000, 7,000 were killed in battle and another 4,000 died of disease and exposure. When Austria joined the Sixth Coalition in 1813 he became commander-in-chief of the allied Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, about 230,000 men. His army was mauled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 26 and 27 August, but beat pursuing French forces at the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a> on 17 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> on 16 to 18 October 1813 Schwarzenberg directed the allied army with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a> and Emperor Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1814 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-sur-aube">Bar-sur-Aube</a> on 27 February, repelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at Arcis-sur-Aube on 20 and 21 March and won at Fère-Champenoise on 30 March. Paris was captured on 31 March, and Napoleon abdicated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> on 11 April. Schwarzenberg's health declined afterwards: a stroke disabled him in 1817, and a second struck him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, where he died on 15 October 1820. Austria held three days of mourning. Alexander I said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> had lost a hero and he a friend.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Austria joined the Sixth Coalition in August 1813, and <b>Field Marshal Karl Philipp</b> zu Schwarzenberg commanded the Allied armies that won the Battle of Leipzig.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The family remained prominent: <b>Karl Philipp zu Schwarzenberg</b> commanded the allied armies at Leipzig in 1813, and Felix zu Schwarzenberg led the Austrian Empire's government after the 1848 revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzenberg">Principality of Schwarzenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karl Philipp, Prince of Schwarzenberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
